I’ll inspect the pattern and metadata first, then use phase-search/indexing and GSAS-II refinement to build a reproducible multiphase model. I’ll validate that the final project, CIFs, profile, fractions, and statistics all agree before writing the report.

The chemistry strongly suggests lead tungstate formation, but I’m treating unreacted oxides and PbWO₄ polymorphs as live possibilities. I’m now extracting peak positions and checking the installed crystallographic search/refinement backends so phase assignment rests on the full pattern, not composition alone.

Peak positions now separate into three coherent families: scheelite PbWO₄, monoclinic WO₃, and orthorhombic PbO (massicot). Several strong reflections are uniquely diagnostic—for example the WO₃ triplet near 23–25° and massicot peaks near 29.35/30.62/38.09°—so I’m carrying all three into quantitative refinement and will test whether each remains statistically and physically supported.

The three-phase model is quantitatively supported, but an unconstrained profile-width trial drove a Gaussian width coefficient negative. I’m tightening the refinement strategy and testing alternative WO₃/PbO structure entries; this is important because phase fractions can otherwise absorb profile-model errors.

The four expected equilibrium/metastable phases all refine to sensible cells and nonzero fractions. The difference profile still has a reproducible extra peak family, especially near 25.91° and 31.05°, so I’m searching the allowed Pb–W–O structure database against the residual before deciding whether to report a fifth crystalline phase or leave it explicitly unidentified.

The refinement rejects raspite (its scale collapses to zero), while both PbO polymorphs are independently required: omitting litharge raises Rwp from 14.38% to 14.66%, and omitting massicot raises it to 16.74%. I’m doing the last structure-choice check on the WO₃ polymorph and then will freeze a conservative model; the remaining isolated 25.91° residual does not match any chemically credible candidate without implausible cell distortion.

The phase assignment is stable at four crystalline phases. I’m now generating the full-range final GSAS-II project and exports, then I’ll validate that the CIFs, profile, fractions, and reported statistics all agree with that project.

The full-range refinement converged cleanly (`Rwp = 14.37%`, 37 refined variables). The modeled crystalline fraction is about 74.34% scheelite PbWO₄, 12.71% monoclinic WO₃, 10.37% massicot PbO, and 2.58% litharge PbO; I’m checking the exported files and crystallographic consistency before writing the uncertainty note.

Three CIFs validate directly. The litharge export exposed a crystallographic issue: its asymmetric-unit coordinates were being interpreted with the wrong multiplicity, yielding Pb₂O/P4/mmm instead of PbO/P4/nmm. I’m correcting that phase definition in the GSAS-II project and will rerun the final refinement so the project and CIF remain the same model.

The corrected litharge model now validates as PbO/P4/nmm and improves the fit to `Rwp = 13.58%`; its fraction rises to 5.37%, showing why that check mattered. I’m doing one final convergence pass and re-evaluating the residual peaks so the uncertainty statement is based on the corrected model.

Leave-one-out refinements confirm that every reported phase is supported independently: omitting PbWO₄, WO₃, massicot, or litharge raises `Rwp` from 13.58% to 37.51%, 14.88%, 16.10%, and 14.65%, respectively. I’m making one last fixed-cell screen of plausible minor Pb–W–O candidates against the corrected four-phase base before finalizing the report.

The minor-phase screen does not justify adding a fifth structure: raspite PbWO₄, WO₂, Pb₇W₈O₂₈.₈, and corundum refine to zero; Pb₃O₄, Pb₂O₃, and W₃O₈ give only small improvements with poor phase-specific fits (`Rf² ≈ 64–71%`), and the reduced tungsten oxide conflicts with the oxidizing precursor context. I’m recording that ambiguity explicitly rather than reporting a spurious fraction.

Completed and validated the four-phase refinement:

- PbWO₄ scheelite: 72.45 wt%
- γ-WO₃ monoclinic: 11.99 wt%
- PbO massicot: 10.19 wt%
- PbO litharge: 5.37 wt%

Fit: `Rwp = 13.577%`, `Rp = 9.165%`, GOF `= 3.886`. No fifth phase could be assigned reliably; fractions are normalized over the modeled crystalline phases.

